{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And He said to me, Behold, I will make you fruitful and multiply you, etc.</b> Why did Jacob change the expression? For there it is stated (Genesis 35:11), ״וַיֹּאמֶר לוֹ אֱלֹהִים אֲנִי אֵל שַׁדַּי פְּרֵה וּרְבֵה גּוֹי וּקְהַל גּוֹיִם יִהְיֶה מִמֶּךָּ״, and behold, in place of ״פְּרֵה וּרְבֵה״ he said ״מַפְרְךָ וְהִרְבִּיתִךָ״ — is this not a matter requiring explanation? And the explanation is that the expression ״פְּרֵה וּרְבֵה״ implies a commandment upon Jacob that he himself engage in procreation, and through this there would emerge from him a nation and a congregation of nations; but here, perforce, the meaning cannot be so, for no son was born to him after this prophecy, and Benjamin's wife was already pregnant — and behold, ״גּוֹי״ he said concerning Benjamin, and ״קְהַל גּוֹיִם״ concerning the one tribe that was destined to split into two, and all this does not depend on his actions through his engaging in procreation. Therefore, in place of פְּרֵה וּרְבֵה, he said ״הִנְנִי מַפְרְךָ וְהִרְבִּיתִךָ״, to say that this is not a commandment upon him that he engage in procreation, but rather the Holy One, blessed be He, Himself makes fruitful and multiplies those already born. And one should not say that this blessing — that those already born would be fruitful and multiply — has nothing to do with one tribe splitting into two; for if so, when He said ״פְּרֵה וּרְבֵה״, which He interpreted as ״מַפְרְךָ״, He should simply have said ״מַפְרְךָ״; rather, the reason He said ״מַפְרְךָ״ in the language of ״פְּרֵה״ is to tell you that that tribe which is destined to split into two would be equivalent in all their matters as though you yourself had begotten them — I mean that they would be called by your name, as though you yourself had begotten them, as it is written (Genesis 48:5), ״וְעַתָּה שְׁנֵי בָנֶיךָ וְגוֹ׳ לִי הֵם״." "And from this Jacob learned that the statement ״פְּרֵה וּרְבֵה״ was not a commandment upon him to engage in procreation, through which he would become a nation and a congregation of nations — for from that prophecy onward no son was born to him through his engaging in procreation. Rather, surely this verse is a blessing and a gift to Jacob to make fruitful and to multiply those already born, and therefore He said that its meaning is ״מַפְרְךָ וּמַרְבְּךָ״. And the meaning is not merely a simple blessing to make his seed fruitful, for if so, why did He not say to him explicitly ״מַפְרְךָ וְהִרְבִּיתִיךָ״? Rather, surely He suspended the matter upon Himself, as if to say: that multiplication is as though I Myself begot them. And what difference does it make that they would be considered as though I begot them? Rather, surely with regard to the matter that they would be called by My name — for each one to be a tribe in its own right. And this is a gift and not by way of obligation. Therefore Jacob said ״וּנְתַתִּיךָ לִקְהַל עַמִּים״, even though this expression was not stated there [in the original prophecy]; nevertheless, all this may be understood from there." "And the reason he called Joseph קָהָל more than the other tribes is that the expression קָהָל indicates that many people are gathered together in one place, and in a place where there is a large gathering, the evil eye is found there more; therefore people refrain from gathering. Except for the tribe of Joseph, for gathering does not harm them, because the evil eye does not harm them, as they learned (Berakhot 20a) from the verse ״בֵּן פֹּרָת יוֹסֵף בֵּן פֹּרָת עֲלֵי עָיִן״ (Genesis 49:22) — read it ״עוֹלֵי עָיִן״, those who rise above the eye." "And Maharai [Ibn Ezra] explained that He made a sign from ״הִנְנִי מַפְרְךָ״ concerning Ephraim, that he too would be a head [of a tribe], for Manasseh the firstborn was in any case head of a tribe, and there was no need to seek a sign except concerning Ephraim. He also brought a sign for this, that it happened that Rachel was buried on the road to Ephrath (Genesis 35:19), for Ephrath alludes to the name Ephraim — and it is a sure sign that Ephraim too would be head of a tribe."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 48, :notes {3 "מַהֲרִי״א resolved as מהר״א אבן עזרא (Ibn Ezra), consistent with the content attributing a peshat reading; the acronym מהר״י would point to Rabbi Isaac of Corbeil or similar, but the Ibn Ezra attribution fits the context."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:34:19.267613098Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}